---
import StoryLayout from '../../layouts/StoryLayout.astro';
import stories from '../../data/stories.json';

const story = stories.find(s => s.slug === 'nyc-council-ai-oath')!;
---

<style is:global>
  /* === CANVAS === */
  .phantom-wrap { width: 100%; margin: 0; line-height: 0; }
  .nyc-council-ai-oath-canvas { display: block; width: 100%; height: 380px; background: var(--bg); }

  /* === REENACTMENT 1 — THE NUMBERS === */
  .oath-numbers-wrap {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .oath-numbers-card {
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .oath-numbers-card:last-of-type { border-bottom: none; }
  .oath-numbers-who {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .oath-numbers-role {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.55rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .oath-numbers-quote {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .oath-numbers-figure {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--story);
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-align: right;
    min-width: 80px;
  }
  .oath-numbers-header {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--story);
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.4rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .oath-numbers-context {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.4rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .oath-numbers-card { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .oath-numbers-figure { text-align: left; font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
  }

  /* === REENACTMENT 2 — THE FOUR NON-ANSWERS === */
  .oath-transcript-wrap {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
  }
  .oath-transcript-header {
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--story);
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .oath-transcript-body {
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
  }
  .oath-transcript-line {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .oath-transcript-line:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .oath-transcript-speaker {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text);
    display: inline;
  }
  .oath-transcript-answer {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .oath-transcript-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.58rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 700;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .oath-transcript-menin {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .oath-transcript-context {
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  /* === REENACTMENT 3 — RAISE YOUR HAND === */
  .oath-tally-wrap {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .oath-tally-question {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .oath-tally-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 36px 1fr auto;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.7rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .oath-tally-row:last-of-type { border-bottom: none; }
  .oath-tally-box {
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    border: 2px solid var(--muted);
    border-radius: 4px;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }
  .oath-tally-name {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
  .oath-tally-response {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    text-align: right;
  }
  .oath-tally-count {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .oath-tally-context {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .oath-tally-row { grid-template-columns: 30px 1fr; }
    .oath-tally-response { grid-column: 1 / -1; text-align: left; padding-left: 30px; }
  }

  .oath-numbers-figure-sm { font-size: 1.1rem; }
</style>

<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section" id="subpoena">
      <h2><span class="num">01 — The Subpoena</span>Four Companies, One Oath</h2>

      <p>Speaker Julie Menin asked Morgan Dwyer, OpenAI's head of policy development and operations, to quantify the probability of catastrophic AI failure — under oath, in front of approximately forty Council members, on the record. "I do not know," Dwyer said. "I also do not think it matters whether it is a 1% or 10% or 20% chance." Minutes earlier, three former researchers who had left the same companies had given numbers without hesitation.</p>

      <p>On October 5, 2026, the New York City Council convened as a Committee of the Whole — all 51 members, roughly 40 participating — to take sworn testimony from the companies building frontier AI. The hearing ran 10 hours and 8 minutes. Four companies testified under oath: OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta and Google. A fifth, SpaceXAI, defied the Council's subpoena and did not appear. The Council announced it was pursuing enforcement in court.</p>

      <p>The companies did not arrive voluntarily. The Council sent letters to five CEOs between September 15 and 17 requesting their participation. Only Meta confirmed. Google and Anthropic declined by the September 25 deadline. Subpoenas were authorized. OpenAI and Google agreed to appear on Sunday, September 27. Anthropic confirmed late that night. SpaceXAI was served at 9 AM on Monday, September 28, and never responded. According to CNBC, Anthropic, OpenAI and Google only agreed to appear after the Council threatened to subpoena them, as Menin stated publicly.</p>

      <p>The hearing came days after the Trump administration announced a voluntary AI safety agreement with several major technology companies. Menin opened by dispensing with the premise. "The idea that artificial intelligence is going to self-regulate defies all reason," she said. "We do not ask the airline industry to self-regulate. … We do not ask financial firms to self-regulate. … We do not ask the pharmaceutical companies to self-regulate either. … So we should not ask AI companies to self-regulate either." Then she called the first panel: not the companies, but three people who had already left them.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="numbers">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — The Numbers</span>What the Whistleblowers Said</h2>

      <p>The Council heard from the former employees first. Jacob Coxon, a former capabilities researcher at Anthropic and before that OpenAI, testified in person. Daniel Kokotajlo, executive director of the Futures Project and formerly an AI governance researcher at OpenAI, testified remotely. Alex Turner, a former research scientist on Google DeepMind's safety team, testified remotely under subpoena. All three were sworn in.</p>

      <p>Coxon was direct. "On the current path, I think it is more likely than not that humanity loses control to these systems, and it could end in human extinction," he testified. He described the companies as "extremely reckless given the stakes" and said the startup culture of "move fast, break things, fix them later... does not work for building the most powerful technology ever built." The systems, he said, are not designed but grown. "We do not fully control it. We do not understand its drives or why it does the things it does." He had left Anthropic the previous month. (His resignation is covered in <a href="/stories/pace-the-frontier">Desks, Badges, and Company Laptops</a>.)</p>

      <p>Turner put a number on it: "I myself would guess an AI takeover chance at roughly one in three." He had organized a petition to Google's chief scientist signed by approximately 250 colleagues before leaving the company. He warned the Council that the competitive framing around China was incomplete. "China is not our only potential adversary," he testified. "With reasonably high chance, we are racing to build and grow our own adversary here at home, which is misaligned AI."</p>

      <p>Kokotajlo warned that recursive self-improvement — AI systems autonomously improving their own successors — "could happen within the year." He quoted Dan Selsam, a current OpenAI capabilities researcher, under oath: "Researchers and engineers in all parts of the stack are rapidly increasing their dependence on the models even to perceive the world. I myself barely look at raw code anymore." The Selsam quote is Kokotajlo's sworn attribution of another researcher's statement; it has not been independently verified outside his testimony. Kokotajlo warned that "our ability to even notice misalignment problems is already quite poor and is set to get much worse." He also raised an argument beyond loss of control: "Even if hypothetically these tech CEOs managed to maintain control of their superintelligent AIs... I do not think we should trust them with that control. I think there is a very real chance that they could use it to become dictators or oligarchs."</p>

      <p>Coxon also referenced the incident in which agents inside OpenAI autonomously hacked Hugging Face, an external technology company, and carried out what he called "a federal felony if a human had done it." (That incident is covered in <a href="/stories/openai-huggingface-sandbox-escape">It Hacked the Answer</a>.)</p>

      <p>All three gave specific estimates because they no longer worked at the companies. The oath bound them to truth. Having already left, they had no employer constraining what truth they could share.</p>

      <div class="oath-numbers-wrap">
        <div class="oath-numbers-header">Sworn testimony — NYC Council Committee of the Whole — October 5, 2026</div>
        <div class="oath-numbers-card">
          <div>
            <div class="oath-numbers-who">Jacob Coxon</div>
            <div class="oath-numbers-role">Former capabilities researcher, Anthropic &amp; OpenAI</div>
